  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124593,
      "id": "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a",
      "title": "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "893574d4-9cbf-5f01-bed3-ede8b0514f2f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 3 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(3 x e^{x y} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(3 t\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 3 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(3 x e^{x y} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2 t^{2}$ and $y=3 t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\left(2 x + 3 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(3 x e^{x y} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\Big|_{x=2 t^{2},\\,y=3 t} \\\\  & =\\left(t \\left(4 t + 9 e^{6 t^{3}}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(6 t \\left(t e^{6 t^{3}} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(3 t\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\left(4 t\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(3\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(t \\left(4 t + 9 e^{6 t^{3}}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(6 t \\left(t e^{6 t^{3}} + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4 t\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(3\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(t \\left(4 t + 9 e^{6 t^{3}}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(4 t\\right)+\\left(6 t \\left(t e^{6 t^{3}} + 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(16 t^{3} + 36 t^{2} e^{6 t^{3}}\\right)+\\left(18 t^{2} e^{6 t^{3}} + 18 t\\right) \\\\  & =2 t \\left(8 t^{2} + 27 t e^{6 t^{3}} + 9\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]\\Big|_{t=-1} & =2 t \\left(8 t^{2} + 27 t e^{6 t^{3}} + 9\\right)\\Big|_{t=-1} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{4 \\left(-3 + 2 e^{3}\\right) \\left(3 + 2 e^{3}\\right)}{e^{6}}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{18 \\left(-1 + e\\right) \\left(1 + e\\right) \\left(1 + e + e^{2}\\right) \\left(- e + 1 + e^{2}\\right)}{e^{6}}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\left(-27 + 17 e^{6}\\right)}{e^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
